WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.790
I'm in Basel, in Switzerland.

00:00:01.790 --> 00:00:03.020
The river Rhine runs through here,

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.760
and if you want to get across it,
then there are four ferries.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.040
I mean, there are bridges as well,
but the ferries keep going

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.420
as a tourist attraction,
as tradition,

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.250
and for some folks, an actual way
to get where you want to go.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.960
Also occasionally a fondue restaurant,
because Switzerland.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.680
But these ferries have
no paddles, no oars, no engines,

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:20.080
nothing on board to power them.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.240
Put them in the open ocean or a lake
and they'd just drift.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.830
And yet here, they move.

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:28.150
So here's how they work:

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.480
there is a wire strung across the river
and the ferry is connected to it.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:34.900
There's a little thing called a traveler
that goes back and forth smoothly.

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.830
The ferry does have a rudder for steering,
so when it's time to cross,

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.610
the captain angles the boat against the current,

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:44.390
and uses the force of the river itself to
push the boat across.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.000
Without that cable,
it'd drift downstream, fast.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.280
But since it can't, it just
crosses the river instead.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.150
No gasoline, no human effort,
just the force of the river.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:56.220
Boats like this are called reaction ferries,

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.700
and there are examples all over the world.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.900
Sometimes the design's a little different,
but the principle is always the same.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.430
I only learned about them a few weeks ago,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:08.550
and in hindsight, they seem incredibly obvious.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.850
You have an object that needs energy

00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:13.670
sitting on top of a
massive, constant energy source

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:15.000
just waiting to be harnessed.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.050
But if you'd asked me how to connect
those two things before I knew the answer,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.340
then my first thought would probably have
been some overcomplicated, overengineered plan

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:25.460
like putting a waterwheel
on the side of the river

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.800
and then using that cable to send power to
an electric motor on the boat.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:32.390
I mean, there is a hydroelectric dam
not far away.

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:35.090
I'd like to think that I'd have
worked it out eventually,

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.680
but I'd have probably missed
the easy option to start with.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.320
To anyone who grew up in a place
with these ferries, it's a really obvious idea.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.290
But to everyone else, it's an invention
we've never thought of.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.240
To be fair, this is only a good solution in
a few places.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.980
You need a strong, fast, steady river,
like the Rhine, at a place where it isn't tidal,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.250
and where it isn't particularly seasonal either.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.580
But if I've missed that, then the obvious
follow-up question is, well:

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:01.580
what else have I missed?

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:05.420
And is there anything out there that
every single human has missed?

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:11.390
Maybe there is still some big, obvious-in-hindsight
stuff out there that'll change the world...

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:13.380
just as soon as someone thinks of it.

